<span class="ltx_personname">USA
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">T-4
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Los Alamos National Laboratory
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Los Alamos
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">NM
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">USA
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) exhibit impressive reasoning and question-answering capabilities. However, they often produce inaccurate or unreliable content known as hallucinations. This unreliability significantly limits their deployment in high-stakes applications. Thus, there is a growing need for a general-purpose method to detect hallucinations in LLMs. In this work, we introduce HalluField, a novel field-theoretic approach for hallucination detection based on a parametrized variational principle and thermodynamics. Inspired by thermodynamics, HalluField models an LLM’s response to a given query and temperature setting as a collection of discrete likelihood token paths, each associated with a corresponding energy and entropy. By analyzing how energy and entropy distributions vary across token paths under changes in temperature and likelihood, HalluField quantifies the semantic stability of a response. Hallucinations are then detected by identifying unstable or erratic behavior in this energy landscape. HalluField is computationally efficient and highly practical: it operates directly on the model’s output logits without requiring fine-tuning or auxiliary neural networks. Notably, the method is grounded in a principled physical interpretation, drawing analogies to the first law of thermodynamics. Remarkably, by modeling LLM behavior through this physical lens, HalluField achieves state-of-the-art hallucination detection performance across models and datasets.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10753v1/figs/hallufield_intuition.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:543/290;" width="543" height="290" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Schematic of the HalluField framework for analyzing LLM responses: The change in free energy, <math id="S1.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔽</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and entropy, <math id="S1.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, between the generated token sequence of interest <math id="S1.F1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{r_{i}=r^{0}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r_{i}=r^{0}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> at the base temperature <math id="S1.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> (tokens along the <span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">blue</span> path) and the sequence generated at a perturbed temperature <math id="S1.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T+\Delta T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T+\Delta T</annotation></semantics></math> (tokens along the <span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red</span> path) are our proposed signatures for hallucination detection. <math id="S1.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝔽</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.F1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th token, formulated in equation <a href="#S3.E7.2" title="Equation 7b ‣ Equation 7 ‣ 3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7b</span></a>, is shown by purple arrows for <math id="S1.F1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <math id="S1.F1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ℍ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.F1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th token with entropy defined in equation <a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>b, is shown by green arrows.
Violin-plots of the total <math id="S1.F1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.F1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> for hallucinated versus non-hallucinated responses at different temperatures (evaluated on LLaMa-2-7B-Chat in the TriviaQA dataset) illustrate why combining these quantities is a promising approach for hallucination detection.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hallucination is a critical and persistent challenge in the use of LLMs. These models, while demonstrating remarkable capabilities across diverse domains, including medicine, education, and software development, are prone to generating outputs that are factually incorrect or logically inconsistent. These hallucinations undermine trust and reliability, especially in high-stakes applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Current efforts to mitigate hallucinations in LLMs primarily rely on uncertainty estimation or probabilistic approaches (see Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries and related Work ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). As LLMs often exhibit well-calibrated predictive confidence, high uncertainty in structured tasks, such as multiple-choice question answering, can serve as a useful proxy for identifying potential hallucinations. However, these approaches face significant limitations. The probabilistic outputs of LLMs are typically high-dimensional, while labeled examples of hallucinations are scarce. This imbalance, often involving only a few thousand hallucinated examples compared to probability vectors with dimensionalities ranging from <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, makes it extremely challenging to extract meaningful and reliable signals for detection. Particularly, existing methods often resort to coarse statistical measures, such as the log-probability of generating the correct answer given a reference <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textup{true}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textup{true}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kadavath et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, the entropy of an ensemble of perturbed model outputs on the same query <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farquhar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, or related variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nikitin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. While these signals can be indicative, they capture only a fraction of the model’s internal fingerprint. Much of the rich structure in the LLM’s response is discarded due to its high complexity, despite its potential to significantly enhance hallucination detection performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of this work is to design a theoretical framework to model the response behavior of LLMs in a way that captures the rich information embedded in their output logits. We demonstrate the effectiveness of this framework through its strong performance in hallucination detection. Motivated by principles from thermodynamics, our approach applies parameterized variational principles to model an LLM’s response to a given query. Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides an intuition of our approach: at a specified temperature, the response is represented as a collection of discrete token likelihood paths <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, each associated with a corresponding energy <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and entropy <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. This formulation enables a principled interpretation and quantification of the structure in LLM outputs, resulting in strong signatures for hallucination detection as illustrated in the violin plots for hallucinated versus non-hallucinated responses in the figure. Another important feature that distinguishes HalluField from state-of-the-art detection methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farquhar et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nikitin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is that the computation of energy and entropy does not require the use of auxiliary LLMs. This design not only reduces computational overhead but also avoids the additional uncertainty and potential errors introduced by relying on extra models, which is critical in high-stakes applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Organization.</span> In <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries and related Work ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we provide background and discuss preliminaries and related work, particularly in the context of hallucination and uncertainty in LLMs, as well as classical variational principles. In <a href="#S3" title="3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, inspired by thermodynamics and variational principles, we develop a theoretical framework for characterizing the responses of LLMs; particularly, we develop notions of energy, entropy, and variations in the LLM setting. This framework allows us to construct the HalluField algorithm for detecting hallucinations, discussed in <a href="#S4" title="4 HalluField Algorithm ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. In <a href="#S5" title="5 Experimental Results ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we demonstrate our proposed method on a variety of large language models and datasets, which show excellent performance with respect to Area Under the Curve (AUC), accuracy, and run time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries and related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Autoregressive Generation in LLMs:</span> An LLM generates textual sequences by autoregressively modeling the conditional probabilities over a discrete token space. Given a query <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, the LLM produces an output token sequence <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tau_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tau_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each token <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to a finite vocabulary <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The generation process is governed by the joint probability <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{i=1}^{N}P\bigl(\tau_{i}\mid Q,\tau_{&lt;i}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{i=1}^{N}P\bigl(\tau_{i}\mid Q,\tau_{&lt;i}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, where the model recursively estimates the next token conditioned on all previous tokens and the input query. At each step <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, the LLM outputs a logit vector <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i}=f_{\theta}(Q,\tau_{&lt;i})\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathbb{T}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝕋</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i}=f_{\theta}(Q,\tau_{&lt;i})\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathbb{T}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is transformed into a probability distribution via the softmax function modulated by a temperature parameter <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\tau_{i}\mid Q,\tau_{&lt;i})=\mathrm{softmax}\left({\ell_{i}}/{T}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\tau_{i}\mid Q,\tau_{&lt;i})=\mathrm{softmax}\left({\ell_{i}}/{T}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The temperature <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> controls the sharpness of the distribution as higher values of <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> lead to more random outputs. After that, the generated token will be selected by sampling from <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\tau_{i}\mid Q,\tau_{&lt;i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\tau_{i}\mid Q,\tau_{&lt;i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the stochastic nature of the generation process, we characterize the LLM’s response as a sequence of tokens parameterized by the temperature <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and a vector of likelihood ranks <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}\in(\mathbb{Z}^{+})^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}\in(\mathbb{Z}^{+})^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each component <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that the <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> token in the sequence corresponds to the <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> most likely token under the model’s predicted distribution at that step. We denote the resulting sequence by <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T)\in\mathbb{T}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T)\in\mathbb{T}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{1},T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{1},T)</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the case where the model always selects the most likely token at all steps. However, due to randomness introduced by temperature-scaled sampling, the model may select lower-ranked tokens. We denote a specific sequence generated by the model at temperature <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> records the rank of each selected token. The LLM’s response that we aim to evaluate for hallucination is referred to as the <span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">base response</span> <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as the specific output generated at the <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">base temperature</span> <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hallucination and Uncertainty in LLMs:</span> Hallucination has become an active area of research, with several detection methods proposed to quantify or mitigate model uncertainty. Early work, such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kadavath et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, introduced the <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textup{true}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>true</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textup{true}}</annotation></semantics></math> measure, which leverages model probabilities as an indicator of correctness. More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farquhar et al. (2024)</a></cite> proposed <em id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Semantic Entropy</em> (SE), which measures uncertainty by clustering semantically equivalent generations and computing entropy over these clusters. Given a query <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, the SE of <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SE_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SE_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle SE_{Q}=-\sum_{C\in\Omega}P(C\mid Q)\log P(C\mid Q)=-\sum_{C\in\Omega}\left(\sum_{s\in C}P(s\mid Q)\right)\log\left(\sum_{s\in C}P(s\mid Q)\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle SE_{Q}=-\sum_{C\in\Omega}P(C\mid Q)\log P(C\mid Q)=-\sum_{C\in\Omega}\left(\sum_{s\in C}P(s\mid Q)\right)\log\left(\sum_{s\in C}P(s\mid Q)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all semantic clusters and <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is the model’s response. In practice, SE estimates <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SE_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SE_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> by querying another LLM and employing a Rao-Blackwellized Monte Carlo estimator, i.e., <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}(C_{i}\mid Q)\approx{P(C_{i}\mid Q)}/{(\sum_{j}P(C_{j}\mid Q))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}(C_{i}\mid Q)\approx{P(C_{i}\mid Q)}/{(\sum_{j}P(C_{j}\mid Q))}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Extending this line of work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nikitin et al. (2024)</a></cite> introduced <em id="S2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kernel Language Entropy</em> (KLE), which estimates fine-grained semantic uncertainty using kernel methods, enabling improved semantic clustering.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, a variety of different approaches have been explored to detect hallucinations in LLMs, including leveraging external knowledge to validate generated content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feldman et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, probing and intervening on hidden states <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burns et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and applying fine-tuning strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. A broad body of work has studied uncertainty quantification in sequential and generative models, with many approaches eliciting uncertainty from LLMs via fine-tuning or prompting with prior generations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kadavath et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen &amp; Mueller, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mielke et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maynez et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ganguli et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao &amp; Wang, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuhn et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Our HalluField approach is complementary to these directions and provides an alternative perspective on hallucination detection from a variational perspective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Variational principles:</span> Generally speaking, variational principles describe the state of a system as a stationary point of a functional. Variational principles are a well-studied subject and appear in many contexts and applications, such as minimal surfaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ulrich Dierkes, 2010</a>)</cite>, partial differential equations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marsden &amp; Hughes, 1983</a>)</cite>, optimization and control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bloch, 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">de León et al., 2007</a>)</cite>, and structure-preserving numerical methods known as variational integrators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marsden &amp; West, 2001</a>)</cite>. We only provide a brief discussion here relevant for our purposes; for a more detailed discussion, see the classic texts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abraham &amp; Marsden, 1987</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marsden &amp; Ratiu, 1999</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arnold, 1978</a>)</cite> as well as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tran &amp; Leok, 2025</a>)</cite> for a recent review.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Consider an action functional <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}:\mathcal{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}:\mathcal{C}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose domain is a space of curves <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined by integrating a <em id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">density</em> <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> along the curve,</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}]=\int A(\mathbf{c})\,ds," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}]=\int A(\mathbf{c})\,ds,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ds" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ds</annotation></semantics></math> is the arclength measure along the curve <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. The classical variational principle seeks to find an extremal curve <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math> which is stationary with respect to variations <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math> of the action:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=\Delta\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}]=\int\frac{\delta A(\mathbf{c})}{\delta\mathbf{c}}ds,\quad\textup{where }\frac{\delta A(\mathbf{c})}{\delta\mathbf{c}}:=\frac{\partial A(\mathbf{c})}{\partial\mathbf{c}}\cdot\delta\mathbf{c}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=\Delta\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}]=\int\frac{\delta A(\mathbf{c})}{\delta\mathbf{c}}ds,\quad\textup{where }\frac{\delta A(\mathbf{c})}{\delta\mathbf{c}}:=\frac{\partial A(\mathbf{c})}{\partial\mathbf{c}}\cdot\delta\mathbf{c}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.3" class="ltx_p">The classical equations of motion seek a stationary point of the function <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial A/\partial\mathbf{c}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial A/\partial\mathbf{c}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Variational framework to study responses of LLMs</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our fundamental quantity for detecting LLM hallucinations will be the change in the <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">internal energy functional</em> on the space of sequences of tokens. According to the first law of thermodynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Landau &amp; Lifshitz, 1980</a>)</cite>, the <em id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">total variation</em> in the internal energy functional <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{U}=T\delta\mathbb{H}+W," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕌</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{U}=T\delta\mathbb{H}+W,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the temperature, <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is the entropy functional, and <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is the work done on the system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In thermodynamics, a system is considered stable if small changes in its state do not cause it to spontaneously move to a different state. In terms of internal energy, stability is closely related to local minima in <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, while sharp increases in <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{U}</annotation></semantics></math> may indicate unstable, short-lived configurations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Landau &amp; Lifshitz, 1980</a>)</cite>. We hypothesize that hallucinated output corresponds to high-energy and less coherent configurations in the token space. Monitoring <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{U}</annotation></semantics></math> across temperatures may thus help identify unreliable responses: <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a hallucinated response whose internal energy is already high, increasing its temperature has little effect on its internal energy, resulting in a low total variation <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, raising the temperature would change a correct, low-energy response into an incorrect, high-energy one, leading to a high <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> While this hypothesis is supported by our experimental results in <a href="#S5" title="5 Experimental Results ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the violin plots in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, and the results in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 HalluField Algorithm ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, additional supporting evidence is provided in greater detail in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Heuristic behaviors of the free energy and the entropy ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach aligns with entropy-based methods such as KLE and SE, which associate high uncertainty with untrustworthy behavior, but it differs in important respects, as will be shown. First, it leverages thermodynamics to systematically compute hallucination signatures across temperatures using the total variation. Second, it captures both entropy and free energy, an intrinsic measure of the reliability of the base response itself, offering a more robust detection signal. Third, it operates directly on response trajectories without relying on auxiliary LLMs, reducing overhead and error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, directly applying equation <a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> to token sequences is intractable as it requires treating entropy as the independent variable and expressing the remaining quantities (temperature and work) as functions of the entropy. In contrast, for LLMs, it is much more convenient to parameterize these quantities in terms of temperature, since temperature can be explicitly controlled by the user. For that purpose, we take the Legendre transform of <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, given by the free energy functional <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Landau &amp; Lifshitz, 1980</a>)</cite>: <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}=\mathbb{U}-T\mathbb{H}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔽</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝕌</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}=\mathbb{U}-T\mathbb{H}.</annotation></semantics></math> Crucially, the free energy functional is now a function of the temperature, which will allow us to construct a formula to calculate its variation. Subsequently, the quantity of interest <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{U}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{U}=\delta\mathbb{F}+\delta(T\mathbb{H})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕌</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{U}=\delta\mathbb{F}+\delta(T\mathbb{H}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Evaluating equation <a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> for LLM’s responses still requires several key ingredients: the free energy functional <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, the entropy functional <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a framework to compute the total variations <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> of functionals defined on the space of token sequences as the temperature varies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Free energy functional <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> In physical systems, the free energy functional <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <span id="S3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">useful</span> portion of energy, i.e., the amount of work that can be extracted under constraints of temperature and entropy. In the LLM setting, <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}</annotation></semantics></math> can be considered as a measure of sequence coherence and confidence, based on the conditional probabilities of generated tokens. Analogous to how free energy is defined over the probability distribution of physical microstates in statistical thermodynamics, we formulate <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}</annotation></semantics></math> for LLMs as a scalar functional over the conditional probabilities of the output token sequence <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>. This formulation satisfies key properties such as <span id="S3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">linearity</span>, <span id="S3.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">monotonicity</span>, and <span id="S3.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">positivity</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Landau &amp; Lifshitz, 1980</a>)</cite>, leading to the following form (details in <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 The free energy functional ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E7.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{F}_{Q}(\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{F}_{Q}(\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{N}F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{N}F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E7.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\log P(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T)|\{\tau_{j}(r_{j},T)\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\log P(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T)|\{\tau_{j}(r_{j},T)\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where the probability in equation <a href="#S3.E7.2" title="Equation 7b ‣ Equation 7 ‣ 3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7b</span></a> is the parameterized version of the probability in equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2 Preliminaries and related Work ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Entropy functional <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> On the other hand, <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the uncertainty of the generated token sequences (details in <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 The entropy functional ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>). High entropy corresponds to a broad distribution over plausible next tokens, which encourages diversity but also increases the risk of hallucinated content.</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E8.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}_{Q}(\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}_{Q}(\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\sum_{i=1}^{N}H_{Q}(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\sum_{i=1}^{N}H_{Q}(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E8.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{Q}(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{Q}(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\sum_{r=1}^{|\mathbb{T}|}P(\tau_{i}(r,T)|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)\times\log P(\tau_{i}(r,T)|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝕋</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\sum_{r=1}^{|\mathbb{T}|}P(\tau_{i}(r,T)|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)\times\log P(\tau_{i}(r,T)|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8b)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Note that, different from SE and related methods (equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2 Preliminaries and related Work ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), our entropy formulation is computed directly at the token level (not the response’s level) and does not require an auxiliary LLM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Total variation <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> The total variation describes how to aggregate the variations along the varied parameters. The details of the construction of the variation are provided in <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Parametrized variations ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>, where we develop a parametrized discrete variation focusing on variations with respect to LLM’s temperature. Combined with <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, these ingredients enable the explicit computation of equation <a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, as will be described in <a href="#S4" title="4 HalluField Algorithm ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Here, we summarize the correspondence between the proposed quantities in the continuous (conventional), discrete (token space), and parametrized discrete variational principles, which highlights our approach in formulating the total variation <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the correspondence among those variational principles for an arbitrary functional <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can either be the free energy or the temperature-entropy functional. In the continuous case, trajectories are described by smooth curves, and the action functional <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}]</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by integration over such trajectories. When passing to the discrete setting, the trajectory becomes a sequence of discrete tokens <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the integral is replaced by a finite sum, leading to a discrete action <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝝉</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}]</annotation></semantics></math>. In the parameterized discrete formulation, the sequence explicitly depends on an external parameter, the temperature <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Accordingly, variations are taken with respect to changes in this parameter, resulting in temperature-dependent difference quotients. Additionally, we parameterize the sequence by the likelihood rank <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, since this information is also provided by the LLM and will be useful for computing our signatures. Thus, the continuous calculus of variations has direct analogues in discrete token sequences, and further generalizes to parameterized variations that capture model dynamics under external controls such as temperature.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Correspondence between quantities in the continuous, discrete, and parametrized discrete variations of a functional.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">Continuous</th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">Discrete</th>
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">Parametrized discrete</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T)</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝝉</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T)]</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\bm{\chi}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝝌</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\bm{\chi}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∫</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\delta A(\mathbf{c})}{\delta\mathbf{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\delta A(\mathbf{c})}{\delta\mathbf{c}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{A_{Q}(\tau_{i})-A_{Q}(\chi_{i})}{d(\tau_{i},\chi_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{A_{Q}(\tau_{i})-A_{Q}(\chi_{i})}{d(\tau_{i},\chi_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T))-A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T))-A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ds" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ds</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Viewed through the lens of variational principles, both the free energy and the entropy, therefore, can be treated as action functionals on sequences of tokens, offering a unified way to analyze LLM dynamics. Particularly, to compute the total variations <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔽</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(T\mathbb{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(T\mathbb{H})</annotation></semantics></math> in equation <a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we define the <em id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">total variation</em> of a functional <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> as a weighted sum over several different variations of that functional <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using temperature as the varied parameter, we heuristically define</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{A}[\bm{\tau}]:=\sum\nolimits_{\Delta T}w(T,\Delta T)\Delta\mathbb{A}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝝉</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{A}[\bm{\tau}]:=\sum\nolimits_{\Delta T}w(T,\Delta T)\Delta\mathbb{A}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is a weight function. Intuitively, the total variation of a functional is a weighted response of the functional to several perturbations in the parameter (in this case, temperature). As opposed to only using a single variation, the total variation provides more complete information on the response of a functional to changes in the external parameter; in the context of hallucination detection, the total variation provides a better picture of how LLM responses, characterized by the free energy and entropy functionals, vary as temperature changes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>HalluField Algorithm</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the theoretical framework established in <a href="#S3" title="3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, we now describe our HalluField algorithm for hallucination detection. In particular, we describe how to compute the total variation of the free energy <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and the total variation of the temperature-entropy functional <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> as a weighted sum over several variations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation of the free energy total variation <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span> We derive a theoretical form of the free energy variation in equation <a href="#A2.E30" title="Equation 30 ‣ B.2 The free energy functional ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a> (<a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 The free energy functional ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>). However, as discussed in more detail in the appendix, the theoretical form is not computable in practice since it requires observing the same LLM’s response at higher temperatures. Thus, we decompose the variation into two computable terms: the base variation <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and the change in potential <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, the base energy variation <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the change of the free energy in the base response <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}^{0}:=\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}^{0}:=\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})</annotation></semantics></math> versus its energy when the temperature is increased:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]=\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0}+\Delta T)]-\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]=\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0}+\Delta T)]-\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">However, when the temperature increment <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T</annotation></semantics></math> is too large, the sequence <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> may never be chosen as the generated tokens. This prevents us from observing <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0}+\Delta T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0}+\Delta T)</annotation></semantics></math> and computing <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0}+\Delta T)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0}+\Delta T)]</annotation></semantics></math>. In such cases, we replace the first term of <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> with the average free energy of the paths generated at that temperature. This yields an approximation of <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{r}}\left[\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T^{0}+\Delta T)]\right]-\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})]\approx\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{r}}\left[\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T^{0}+\Delta T)]\right]-\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})]\approx\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10753v1/figs/metrics_vs_temperatures_with_reversed_auc.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:543/220;" width="543" height="220" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Differences (top) and the AUCs (bottom) of the base energy variation <math id="S4.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the change in potential <math id="S4.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the change in entropy <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> between hallucinated and non-hallucinated responses as a function of temperature for different datasets in LLaMa-2-7B-Chat.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, the change in potential <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> captures the variation in potential that arises when the model generates a different sequence of tokens as a result of the increased temperature:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{r}}\left[\textup{I}(\mathbf{r}\neq\mathbf{r}^{0})\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T^{0}+\Delta T)]-\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})]\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{r}}\left[\textup{I}(\mathbf{r}\neq\mathbf{r}^{0})\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T^{0}+\Delta T)]-\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})]\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{I}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>I</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{I}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the indicator function (1 if the condition is true, 0 otherwise). As noted earlier (see equation <a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), the total variation in free energy, <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined as a weighted sum of the base variation <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and the potential change <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> across multiple <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T</annotation></semantics></math> values, offering a better measure of how the free energy functional varies as opposed to using a single <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{F}_{Q}:=\sum_{\Delta T=\Delta T_{1}}^{\Delta T_{n}}w_{\mathbb{B}}(T^{0};\Delta T)\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]+\sum_{\Delta T=\Delta T_{1}}^{\Delta T_{n}}w_{\mathbb{P}}(T^{0};\Delta T)\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{F}_{Q}:=\sum_{\Delta T=\Delta T_{1}}^{\Delta T_{n}}w_{\mathbb{B}}(T^{0};\Delta T)\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]+\sum_{\Delta T=\Delta T_{1}}^{\Delta T_{n}}w_{\mathbb{P}}(T^{0};\Delta T)\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4 HalluField Algorithm ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the advantage of using weighted sums across variations of the free energy for hallucination detection. At lower temperatures, the potential change yields a stronger detection capability, as reflected by the larger statistical gap in the signatures and higher AUC values. Conversely, base variation becomes more effective at higher temperatures. Our implementation of HalluField uses:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w_{\mathbb{B}}(T^{0};\Delta T)=T^{0}+\Delta T\quad\textup{and}\quad w_{\mathbb{P}}(T^{0};\Delta T)=1/(T^{0}+\Delta T)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>ℙ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w_{\mathbb{B}}(T^{0};\Delta T)=T^{0}+\Delta T\quad\textup{and}\quad w_{\mathbb{P}}(T^{0};\Delta T)=1/(T^{0}+\Delta T)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation of the temperature-entropy total variation <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">𝐂𝐋𝐎𝐒𝐄</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> Similarly, the temperature-entropy functional variation <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is also measured only when the model generates a different sequence of tokens caused by a higher entropy:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})[\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T^{0});\Delta T]=T^{0}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{r}}\left[\textup{I}(\mathbf{r}\neq\mathbf{r}^{0})\mathbb{H}_{Q}(\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T^{0}+\Delta T))-\mathbb{H}_{Q}(\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T^{0}))\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝐫</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>I</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})[\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T^{0});\Delta T]=T^{0}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{r}}\left[\textup{I}(\mathbf{r}\neq\mathbf{r}^{0})\mathbb{H}_{Q}(\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T^{0}+\Delta T))-\mathbb{H}_{Q}(\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T^{0}))\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As in equation <a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, the total variation in the temperature-entropy functional is given by a weighted sum over several variations <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T</annotation></semantics></math>, where the impact of different temperatures is also depicted in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 HalluField Algorithm ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\sum_{\Delta T=\Delta T_{1}}^{\Delta T_{n}}w_{T\mathbb{H}}(T^{0};\Delta T)\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})[\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T^{0});\Delta T]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\sum_{\Delta T=\Delta T_{1}}^{\Delta T_{n}}w_{T\mathbb{H}}(T^{0};\Delta T)\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})[\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T^{0});\Delta T],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\textup{where }w_{T\mathbb{H}}(T^{0};\Delta T)={1}/{(T^{0}+\Delta T)^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\textup{where }w_{T\mathbb{H}}(T^{0};\Delta T)={1}/{(T^{0}+\Delta T)^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HalluField:</span> Altogether, HalluField computes the change in the internal energy equation <a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{U}_{Q}=\delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}+\delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕌</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{U}_{Q}=\delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}+\delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{\Delta T=\Delta T_{1}}^{\Delta T_{n}}\left[(T^{0}+\Delta T)\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]+\frac{\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]}{(T^{0}+\Delta T)^{2}}+\frac{\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})[\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T^{0});\Delta T]}{(T^{0}+\Delta T)^{2}}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝝉</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{\Delta T=\Delta T_{1}}^{\Delta T_{n}}\left[(T^{0}+\Delta T)\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]+\frac{\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}^{0};\Delta T]}{(T^{0}+\Delta T)^{2}}+\frac{\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})[\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T^{0});\Delta T]}{(T^{0}+\Delta T)^{2}}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.2" class="ltx_p">As a final refinement, we incorporate the semantic entropy term <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{SE}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{SE}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> (equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2 Preliminaries and related Work ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), which leads to the HalluFieldSE algorithm:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{HalluFieldSE}=\delta\mathbb{U}_{Q}+\lambda{SE}_{Q}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>HalluFieldSE</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝕌</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{HalluFieldSE}=\delta\mathbb{U}_{Q}+\lambda{SE}_{Q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyper-parameter, set to <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> in our implementation. The signatures <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{U}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝕌</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{U}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S4.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">HalluFieldSE</span> are used as predictors for hallucinations. The resulting methods are referred to as HalluField and HalluFieldSE, respectively. The pseudocode of HalluField is provided in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4 HalluField Algorithm ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> HalluField</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
            
            
           The LLM, query <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, base temperature <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, number of perturbations <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, temperature variations <math id="alg1.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Delta T_{1},\dots,\Delta T_{N}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Delta T_{1},\dots,\Delta T_{N}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
            
            
           Total internal energy variation <math id="alg1.l0a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{U}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
            
            
           Ask <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> to the LLM with base temperature <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>; collect the token probability <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
            
            
           <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}=0</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})=0</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
            
            
           <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> variation <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
            
            
            Ask <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> to the LLM with temperature <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{0}+\Delta T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{0}+\Delta T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; collect the token probability

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta F_{Q}\mathrel{+}=w_{\mathbb{B}}(T^{0};\Delta T_{i})\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}[T^{0};\Delta T_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">+</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>𝔹</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta F_{Q}\mathrel{+}=w_{\mathbb{B}}(T^{0};\Delta T_{i})\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}[T^{0};\Delta T_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">{Use equation <a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 4 HalluField Algorithm ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, equation <a href="#S4.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ 4 HalluField Algorithm ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and equation <a href="#S3.E7.1" title="Equation 7a ‣ Equation 7 ‣ 3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7a</span></a>}
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
            
            
