<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Heuristic behaviors of the free energy and the entropy</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this appendix, we present experimental results illustrating the behavior of our proposed quantity in non-hallucinated and hallucinated responses generated by LLaMA-2 7B-Chat, LLaMA-3.2 3B, and Phi-3 Mini-Inst. The experiments are conducted on the triviaQA and squad datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results are shown in Figures <a href="#A1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix A Heuristic behaviors of the free energy and the entropy ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>–<a href="#A1.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix A Heuristic behaviors of the free energy and the entropy ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. Across different temperatures, models, and datasets, we consistently observe that hallucinated responses yield statistically higher values of all three measures: the base energy variation <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the change in potential <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the change in entropy <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. These findings align with our hypothesis stated in <a href="#S3" title="3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, we observe that the ability to distinguish hallucinated from non-hallucinated responses varies across different temperatures. This observation motivates the design of HalluField, a method that aggregates this rich information into an effective approach for hallucination detection.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10753v1/figs/trivia_qa_meta-llama_llama-2-7b-chat-hf.png" id="A1.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/348;" width="439" height="348" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Behaviors of the base energy variation <math id="A1.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the change in potential <math id="A1.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the change in entropy <math id="A1.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> between non-hallucinated and hallucinated responses of LLaMa-2-7B-Chat in triviaQA dataset</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10753v1/figs/trivia_qa_meta-llama_llama-3.2-3b.png" id="A1.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/348;" width="439" height="348" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Behaviors of the base energy variation <math id="A1.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the change in potential <math id="A1.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the change in entropy <math id="A1.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> between non-hallucinated and hallucinated responses of LLaMa-3.2-3B in triviaQA dataset</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10753v1/figs/trivia_qa_microsoft_phi-3-mini-4k-instruct.png" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/348;" width="439" height="348" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Behaviors of the base energy variation <math id="A1.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the change in potential <math id="A1.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the change in entropy <math id="A1.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> between non-hallucinated and hallucinated responses of Phi-3 Mini-Inst in triviaQA dataset</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10753v1/figs/squad_meta-llama_llama-2-7b-chat-hf.png" id="A1.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/348;" width="439" height="348" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Behaviors of the base energy variation <math id="A1.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the change in potential <math id="A1.F7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the change in entropy <math id="A1.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> between non-hallucinated and hallucinated responses of LLaMa-2-7B-Chat in squad dataset</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10753v1/figs/squad_meta-llama_llama-3.2-3b.png" id="A1.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/348;" width="439" height="348" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Behaviors of the base energy variation <math id="A1.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the change in potential <math id="A1.F8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the change in entropy <math id="A1.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> between non-hallucinated and hallucinated responses of LLaMa-3.2-3B in squad dataset</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10753v1/figs/squad_microsoft_phi-3-mini-4k-instruct.png" id="A1.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/348;" width="439" height="348" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Behaviors of the base energy variation <math id="A1.F9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{B}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the change in potential <math id="A1.F9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{P}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the change in entropy <math id="A1.F9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> between non-hallucinated and hallucinated responses of Phi-3 Mini-Inst in squad dataset</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Variational principle on sequences of tokens</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix provides our formulation of the variation of a functional defined on the space of sequences of tokens; we will subsequently define in <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Parametrized variations ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> a parametrized version of the variation which allows for one to compute variations as a model quantity (e.g., temperature) varies. We then specifically consider a free energy functional (<a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 The free energy functional ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>) and an entropy functional (<a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 The entropy functional ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>) on the space of sequences of tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the space of tokens and <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{T}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{T}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the space of sequences of tokens. Let <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{Q}:\mathbb{T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{Q}:\mathbb{T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, referred to as a <em id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">density</em>; a corresponding functional <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{Q}:\mathbb{T}^{*}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{Q}:\mathbb{T}^{*}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> can be defined by summing the density over the token sequence, i.e.,</p>
<table id="A2.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}]=\sum_{i=1}^{N}A_{Q}[\tau_{i}]\Delta_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝝉</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}]=\sum_{i=1}^{N}A_{Q}[\tau_{i}]\Delta_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of tokens of length <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is the distance between token depths <math id="A2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and the subscript <math id="A2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> throughout denotes dependence on the query <math id="A2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Given another sequence of tokens <math id="A2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\chi}=\{\chi_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝌</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\chi}=\{\chi_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the discrete variation of this functional from <math id="A2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\chi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\chi}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="A2.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\bm{\chi}]=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{A_{Q}(\tau_{i})-A_{Q}(\chi_{i})}{d(\tau_{i},\chi_{i})}\Delta_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>𝝌</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\bm{\chi}]=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{A_{Q}(\tau_{i})-A_{Q}(\chi_{i})}{d(\tau_{i},\chi_{i})}\Delta_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d:\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕋</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d:\mathbb{T}\times\mathbb{T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a pseudo-distance function between tokens <math id="A2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> which is symmetric and non-negative; in order to avoid vanishing divisors in the discrete setting, we impose that the pseudo-distance function is bounded below by <math id="A2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="A2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\tau,\chi)\geq\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\tau,\chi)\geq\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for all tokens <math id="A2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau,\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>χ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau,\chi</annotation></semantics></math>, and the pseudo-non-degeneracy condition <math id="A2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\tau,\chi)=\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\tau,\chi)=\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="A2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>χ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\chi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Parametrized variations</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A simple way to compute the variation of a functional is to parametrize the domain of the functional by parameters and look at variations induced by changing those parameters. Furthermore, such parametrized variations allow one to compute how a functional changes with respect to a particular parameter; for example, the HalluField algorithm uses variations with respect to temperature.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Starting with the continuous case, let us consider a functional <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}</annotation></semantics></math> on a space of curves,</p>
<table id="A2.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r},T)]=\int A(\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r},T))\,ds," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔸</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r},T)]=\int A(\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r},T))\,ds,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where now the curve <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is parametrized by two parameters <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> (in the discrete token setting, this will later be the likelihood depth and the temperature). Note that the parameters (particularly <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math>) may generally depend on the current time <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> along the path.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of considering generic variations of the curve <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, parametrized variations consider only variations of the curve <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by varying the parameters. Particularly, the parametrized variation of this functional with respect to <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="A2.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r},T);\Delta T]=\int\left(\frac{\partial A}{\partial\mathbf{c}}\cdot\frac{\partial\mathbf{c}}{\partial T}\Delta T\right)ds." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔸</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>𝐜</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{A}[\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r},T);\Delta T]=\int\left(\frac{\partial A}{\partial\mathbf{c}}\cdot\frac{\partial\mathbf{c}}{\partial T}\Delta T\right)ds.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Now, on to the setting of tokens. Given any two sequences, we can assume that they are the same length by trivially extending the shorter sequence. We consider a sequence of tokens parametrized by the temperature <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathbb{R}^{\geq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>≥</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathbb{R}^{\geq}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a vector of likelihood depths <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}\in(\mathbb{Z}^{+})^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}\in(\mathbb{Z}^{+})^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> component of this vector <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to taking the <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> most likely token for the <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> token in the sequence. We denote this token sequence by</p>
<table id="A2.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T)\in\mathbb{T}^{*}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T)\in\mathbb{T}^{*}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> token of this sequence is denoted <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}(r_{i},T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}(r_{i},T)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider again a functional on the space of sequences of tokens, <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{Q}:\mathbb{T}^{*}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{Q}:\mathbb{T}^{*}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the parametrized functional</p>
<table id="A2.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T)]:=\sum_{i=1}^{N}A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))\Delta_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T)]:=\sum_{i=1}^{N}A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))\Delta_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Of course, the functional <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r},T)]</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the parameters <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>; when it is clear in context, we will simply express this as <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝝉</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}]</annotation></semantics></math>. In analogy to the discrete variation equation <a href="#A2.E19" title="Equation 19 ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> and continuous parametrized variation equation <a href="#A2.E21" title="Equation 21 ‣ B.1 Parametrized variations ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>, we define the <em id="A2.SS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parametrized discrete variation</em> with respect to a temperature perturbation <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\rightarrow T+\Delta T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\rightarrow T+\Delta T</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T\geq-T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T\geq-T</annotation></semantics></math>) by</p>
<table id="A2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]:=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T))-A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))}{d(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T),\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))}|\Delta T|\Delta_{i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]:=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T))-A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))}{d(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T),\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))}|\Delta T|\Delta_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Based on this notation, we refer to the model’s response that we wish to classify as either a hallucination or a non-hallucination as the <span id="A2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">base response</span>. This corresponds to the path <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterized by the token-likelihood choice <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> at the model’s normal operating temperature <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, the response under evaluation for hallucination can be denoted as <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that under this notation, while <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a different path, it corresponds to the same
sequence of tokens in the base response.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Given a query <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, base temperature <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the corresponding response likelihood path <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math>, HalluField considers a uniform distance between token depths <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i}=1/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i}=1/N</annotation></semantics></math> and uses the following parametrized discrete variation with respect to the temperature:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}|\Delta T|\frac{A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r^{0}_{i},T^{0}+\Delta T))-A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r^{0}_{i},T^{0}))}{d(\tau_{i}(r^{0}_{i},T^{0}+\Delta T),\tau_{i}(r^{0}_{i},T^{0}))}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}|\Delta T|\frac{A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r^{0}_{i},T^{0}+\Delta T))-A_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r^{0}_{i},T^{0}))}{d(\tau_{i}(r^{0}_{i},T^{0}+\Delta T),\tau_{i}(r^{0}_{i},T^{0}))}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Another advantage of parametrizing the variations is that it eliminates the need to compute distances directly between tokens (which could alternatively be computed using embeddings, but at a significantly higher computational cost). Instead, we only require a distance function defined over tokens that differ with respect to the chosen parameter—in this case, the temperature <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. A natural and simplest choice for such a distance is then</p>
<table id="A2.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\tau(r^{0}_{i},T+\Delta T),\tau(r^{0}_{i},T))=|\Delta T|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\tau(r^{0}_{i},T+\Delta T),\tau(r^{0}_{i},T))=|\Delta T|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">This gives the following simplification of the parametrized discrete variation</p>
<table id="A2.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0}+\Delta T)]-\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐫</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0}+\Delta T)]-\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau}(\mathbf{r}^{0},T^{0})].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Total variation.</span> Now, we define the total variation of the functional <math id="A2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> by a weighted sum of the parametrized discrete variations <math id="A2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]</annotation></semantics></math> for various choices of <math id="A2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T=\Delta T_{1},\dots,\Delta T_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T=\Delta T_{1},\dots,\Delta T_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. That is,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}:=\sum_{\Delta T=\Delta T_{1}}^{\Delta T_{n}}w(T,\Delta T)\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}:=\sum_{\Delta T=\Delta T_{1}}^{\Delta T_{n}}w(T,\Delta T)\Delta\mathbb{A}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, the total variation is a linear combination of several individual variations; the choice of a linear combination comes from the fact that variations measure the linear response of a functional to a perturbation in the parameter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>The free energy functional</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a query <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, we aim to define a scalar quantity that captures the essence of a token sequence just like energy captures the essence of a trajectory in classical physics. We want this scalar property to be additive and dependent on the length of the token sequence for a fair comparison between <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> of different lengths and for satisfying linearity defined in equation <a href="#A2.E23" title="Equation 23 ‣ B.1 Parametrized variations ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>. In thermodynamics, such a quantity is called an extensive property. We call this the <em id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">free energy</em> <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{Q}:\mathbb{T}^{*}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{Q}:\mathbb{T}^{*}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and define it as a map such that <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous, monotonic, and a thermodynamically extensive function of the token sequence.
We derive the functional form of this energy from the following statistical arguments (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Landau &amp; Lifshitz (1980)</a></cite>). Let the probability of getting a token sequence <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> from query <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be given by the conditional probability <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{\tau}|Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{\tau}|Q)</annotation></semantics></math>. For the <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> token <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the sequence <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\tau}=\{\tau_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\tau}=\{\tau_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, its probability is conditioned on all previous tokens, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j}^{i-1},Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j}^{i-1},Q)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the following relation holds between the joint probability <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{\tau},Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{\tau},Q)</annotation></semantics></math> and conditional probabilities of all tokens:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(\bm{\tau},Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(\bm{\tau},Q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P(\tau_{1},\tau_{2},...,\tau_{N},Q)=\prod_{i}^{N}P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)P(Q)=P(\bm{\tau}|Q)P(Q)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P(\tau_{1},\tau_{2},...,\tau_{N},Q)=\prod_{i}^{N}P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)P(Q)=P(\bm{\tau}|Q)P(Q).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the extensive property, a free energy defined on the sequence of tokens must be a function of these conditional probabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Landau &amp; Lifshitz, 1980</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{F}_{Q}(\bm{\tau}|Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{F}_{Q}(\bm{\tau}|Q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=F_{Q}\left(\prod_{i}^{N}P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)\right)=\sum_{i}^{N}F_{Q}(P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=F_{Q}\left(\prod_{i}^{N}P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)\right)=\sum_{i}^{N}F_{Q}(P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Equation <a href="#A2.E28" title="Equation 28 ‣ B.2 The free energy functional ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a> is a logarithmic functional equation, which leads to the following family of possible functions via Cauchy’s functional equation:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F_{Q}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F_{Q}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-k\log P(\tau|Q).=-k\log P(\tau|Q)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-k\log P(\tau|Q).=-k\log P(\tau|Q).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F_{Q}(\tau_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F_{Q}(\tau_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-k\log P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)=-\log P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-k\log P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)=-\log P(\tau_{i}|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive real number. As convention, we take <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Its variation is then given by</p>
<table id="A2.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]:=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T))-F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))}{d(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T),\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))}|\Delta T|\Delta_{i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]:=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T))-F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))}{d(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T),\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))}|\Delta T|\Delta_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To explain why the absolute value <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta T|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta T|</annotation></semantics></math> appears, let us check the intuition for the sum appearing in equation <a href="#A2.E24" title="Equation 24 ‣ B.1 Parametrized variations ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>. Consider a perturbation in the temperature. For <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T))&gt;F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T))&gt;F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))</annotation></semantics></math> (since the sequence of tokens generated at lower temperature should have higher probability and hence, lower free energy) which makes the sum positive, i.e., we have a positive variation in the free energy functional. On the other hand, if <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T))&lt;F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T))&lt;F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to a negative variation in the free energy functional. Mathematically, we do not keep track of the sign of the factors of <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in the summands of equation <a href="#A2.E24" title="Equation 24 ‣ B.1 Parametrized variations ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a> since this is already accounted for in the difference of the free energies. Furthermore, <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta T|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta T|</annotation></semantics></math> is the unsigned measure of the interval <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T,T+\Delta T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T,T+\Delta T]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the computation of <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]</annotation></semantics></math> in equation <a href="#A2.E30" title="Equation 30 ‣ B.2 The free energy functional ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a> requires to compute <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T))-F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T+\Delta T))-F_{Q}(\tau_{i}(r_{i},T))</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the difference between the free energy of two exactly generated sequences of tokens but at different temperatures. In practice, this can be challenging since, at a large <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta T</annotation></semantics></math>, the LLM will become too random for us to observe the same response at a lower temperature. We discuss how to approximate this quantity in the description of our algorithm in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 HalluField Algorithm ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>The entropy functional</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us return to the continuous case. To define the entropy functional, we consider a family of curves given by varying <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Namely, for the curve <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r},T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{r},T)</annotation></semantics></math>, for each time <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, we take the parameter <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\mathbf{r}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\mathbf{r}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> to be distributed by some probability distribution <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{c}(r,T)(s))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{c}(r,T)(s))</annotation></semantics></math>. The entropy of this family of curves at time <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be</p>
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(\mathbf{c}(\cdot,T)(s))=-\sum_{r}p(\mathbf{c}(r,T)(s))\log p(\mathbf{c}(r,T)(s))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>r</mi></munder><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathbf{c}(\cdot,T)(s))=-\sum_{r}p(\mathbf{c}(r,T)(s))\log p(\mathbf{c}(r,T)(s)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the sum is over all possible states parametrized by <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="A2.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">entropy functional</em> is given by integrating the entropy over all times <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}(\mathbf{c}(\cdot,T))=\int H(\mathbf{c}(\cdot,T)(s))ds," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℍ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}(\mathbf{c}(\cdot,T))=\int H(\mathbf{c}(\cdot,T)(s))ds,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which is interpreted as the total entropy of this family of parametrized curves.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Analogous to our discussion of the free energy, we can define a discrete analogue of the entropy functional on sequences of tokens using the Shannon entropy,</p>
<table id="A2.E31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody><tr id="A2.EGx19" class="ltx_eqn_row"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell" colspan="5"></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E31.1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E31.1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{H}_{Q}(\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{H}_{Q}(\bm{\tau}(\cdot,T))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E31.1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\sum_{i=1}^{N}H_{Q}(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T))\Delta_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\sum_{i=1}^{N}H_{Q}(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T))\Delta_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31a)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E31.2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E31.2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{Q}(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{Q}(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E31.2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\sum_{r=1}^{|\mathbb{T}|}P(\tau_{i}(r,T)|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝕋</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\sum_{r=1}^{|\mathbb{T}|}P(\tau_{i}(r,T)|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31b)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E31.x1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E31.x1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad\quad\times\log P(\tau_{i}(r,T)|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="3.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad\quad\times\log P(\tau_{i}(r,T)|\{\tau_{j}\}_{j=1}^{i-1},Q).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The definition of the entropy functional, equation <a href="#A2.E31.1" title="Equation 31a ‣ Equation 31 ‣ B.3 The entropy functional ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31a</span></a> and equation <a href="#A2.E31.2" title="Equation 31b ‣ Equation 31 ‣ B.3 The entropy functional ‣ Appendix B Variational principle on sequences of tokens ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31b</span></a>, can be interpreted as a discrete double integral over the length of the token sequence in one direction and over all possible likelihood depths in the other direction (where the likelihood depths run from 1 to the total context length <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbb{T}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝕋</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbb{T}|</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">According to equation <a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we are interested in the parametrized discrete variation of the temperature-entropy functional (which is simply the product of the temperature and the entropy functional). Proceeding similarly to the free energy functional, the parametrized discrete variation of the temperature-entropy functional, with respect to a temperature perturbation <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\rightarrow T+\Delta T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\rightarrow T+\Delta T</annotation></semantics></math>, is given by</p>
<table id="A2.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]:=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{(T+\Delta T)H(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T+\Delta T))-TH(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T))}{d(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T+\Delta T),\tau_{i}(\cdot,T))}|\Delta T|\Delta_{i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝝉</mi><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})[\bm{\tau};\Delta T]:=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{(T+\Delta T)H(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T+\Delta T))-TH(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T))}{d(\tau_{i}(\cdot,T+\Delta T),\tau_{i}(\cdot,T))}|\Delta T|\Delta_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">These parametrized discrete variations of the free energy functional, <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and of the temperature-entropy functional, <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(T\mathbb{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(T\mathbb{H})</annotation></semantics></math>, form the basis of the HalluField algorithm, which considers a weighted combination of these variations to compute the total variation</p>
<table id="A2.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mathbb{U}_{Q}=\delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}+\delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝕌</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mathbb{U}_{Q}=\delta\mathbb{F}_{Q}+\delta(T\mathbb{H}_{Q}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">as described in <a href="#S3" title="3 Variational framework to study responses of LLMs ‣ HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Experimental settings</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments were conducted on a cluster with nodes featuring four NVIDIA Hopper (H100) GPUs each, paired with NVIDIA Grace CPUs via NVLink-C2C for rapid data transfer essential for intensive computational tasks. Each GPU is equipped with 96GB of HBM2 memory, ideal for handling large models and datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each dataset, we follow the evaluation protocol of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farquhar et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nikitin et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and assess hallucination rates on 500 samples. For HalluField, we generate 50 perturbations per temperature. The benchmark methods use a comparable number of perturbations, namely <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\times\textup{Number of temperatures}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>Number of temperatures</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\times\textup{Number of temperatures}</annotation></semantics></math>. We typically use the temperature set <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1.0,1.5,2.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1.0,1.5,2.0\}</annotation></semantics></math>; for models with different temperature scaling such as LLaMa-2-7B-Chat, we instead use <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1.0,2.0,3.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1.0,2.0,3.0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For KLE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nikitin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which integrates multiple parameters and kernel methods, we adopt the strongest reported variant, <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{KLE}_{\text{Heat}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>KLE</mtext><mtext>Heat</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{KLE}_{\text{Heat}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as recommended by the authors. We further fine-tune its parameters and set <math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{KLE}=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{KLE}=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{KLE}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{KLE}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At high temperatures, models tend to generate longer responses, which substantially increases evaluation time since each output must be processed by another LLM to obtain ground-truth labels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farquhar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Following prior work, we cap the number of generated tokens at 50 to control runtime.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
